\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The two eigenspaces of a single direction}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For #{n=1} take #{S=\mathbb C} and #{\varepsilon_1=\bbI_S}.
Coordinate conjugation fixes the direction. Every antiunitary
conjugates the identity to itself, so the negative
[sign](fdip-017H) is unavailable on this line. Add a second
copy with direction #{-\bbI}. The map
#{(z,w)\mapsto(\bar w,\bar z)} on their orthogonal sum exchanges
the two eigenspaces and negates the direction. This is the
two-line decomposition of the flip in \ref{fdip-016Q}.}
